---
import CaseStudy from '../../layouts/CaseStudy.astro';
import Claim from '../../components/Claim.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../../components/CodeBlock.astro';
import TemperatureDemo from '../../components/TemperatureDemo.astro';
import HomeAway from '../../components/HomeAway.astro';
import FalseAlarms from '../../components/FalseAlarms.astro';
import { commitUrl, evidence, type ClaimId } from '../../data/claims';
import { post } from '../../data/content';

const sources: ClaimId[] = [
  'cf2.v1.homeBA', 'cf2.v1.awayBA', 'cf2.v2.homeBA', 'cf2.v2.awayBA', 'cf2.best2018',
  'cf2.v2.delta', 'cf2.v2.deltaCi', 'cf2.v1.homeMelAUC', 'cf2.v2.homeMelAUC', 'cf2.v1.melAUC', 'cf2.v2.melAUC',
  'cf2.v1.malAUC', 'cf2.v2.malAUC', 'cf2.melRecall.deltaCi',
  'cf2.offsets.first', 'cf2.offsets.median',
  'cf2.imgOnly.awayBA', 'cf2.fusion.vsImg', 'cf2.fusion.vsImgCi', 'cf2.fusion.noForm', 'cf2.fusion.form', 'cf2.fusion.formCi',
  'cf2.v1.sens', 'cf2.v2.sens', 'cf2.v1.spec', 'cf2.v2.spec', 'cf2.v1.fpPerTp', 'cf2.v2.fpPerTp',
  'cf2.v2.coverage', 'cf2.v1.setSize', 'cf2.v2.setSize', 'cf2.v2.ece',
  'cf.split', 'cf.test.n', 'cf.test.macroF1',
  'cf.temperature', 'cf.ece.before', 'cf.ece.after', 'cf.latency.explain', 'cf.latency.fast',
];
const toc = [
  { id: 'what', label: 'What it does' },
  { id: 'away', label: 'The test it failed' },
  { id: 'audit', label: 'Reading my own code' },
  { id: 'offsets', label: 'The cheap fix that wasn’t' },
  { id: 'v2', label: 'v2' },
  { id: 'flag', label: 'The malignant flag' },
  { id: 'fusion', label: 'Does the metadata earn the name?' },
  { id: 'silent', label: 'The silent failure' },
  { id: 'calibration', label: 'Calibration' },
  { id: 'latency', label: 'Latency' },
  { id: 'limits', label: 'What this doesn’t show' },
  { id: 'reproduce', label: 'Reproduce' },
];
const blog = post('cancerFusion');
const bug = `state = torch.load("models/best_model.pth")   # stale, image-only
model.load_state_dict(state, strict=False)     # mismatched weights skipped, no error`;
const fixed = `result = model.load_state_dict(state, strict=False)
print(f"missing={len(result.missing_keys)} "
      f"unexpected={len(result.unexpected_keys)}")`;
const meta = `# v1: "unknown" is one more category, learned from a few rows that are all benign
sex_onehot = one_hot(sex, ["female", "male", "unknown"])

# v2: missing is a flag, and training blanks each field at random
vec = [age_scaled, age_missing, *sex_onehot, sex_missing, *site_onehot, site_missing]`;
const fix = commitUrl(evidence.cfCollapseFix.repo, evidence.cfCollapseFix.sha);
const loud = commitUrl(evidence.cfSilentLoad.repo, evidence.cfSilentLoad.sha);
const temperature = commitUrl(evidence.cfTemperature.repo, evidence.cfTemperature.sha);
---
<CaseStudy
  title="Cancer Fusion AI"
  dek="A skin-lesion classifier that lost seven points on someone else's test set, and the rebuild that got them back."
  description="A skin-lesion classifier on HAM10000: external validation on ISIC 2018 exposed a drop, a decision-threshold fix only moved the errors, and a rebuilt v2 raised external balanced accuracy from 0.653 to 0.782."
  year="2026"
  stack={['PyTorch', 'timm', 'EfficientNetV2-S', 'ResNet50', 'conformal prediction', 'temperature scaling', 'Grad-CAM', 'FastAPI', 'React']}
  links={[
    { label: 'Write-up', href: blog.href },
    { label: 'Code', href: 'https://github.com/Avnish1505/cancer-fusion-ai' },
  ]}
  sources={sources}
  toc={toc}
  ogImage="/og/cancer-fusion.png"
>
  <h2 id="what">What it does</h2>
  <p>
    The model sorts a dermoscopy image into one of the seven lesion classes in HAM10000, from harmless moles to melanoma, and can
    also take the patient's age, sex and lesion site. Around it sits a safety layer: calibrated probabilities, a conformal set of
    classes that should contain the true one 90% of the time, a malignant flag tuned to catch 95% of cancers, and a guard that
    refuses images that do not look like dermoscopy. Every threshold is fitted on validation data and tied to the checkpoint's hash.
  </p>
  <p>
    The split is by <Claim id="cf.split" />, not by image. Some lesions have several photos, and an image-level split would put the
    same lesion on both sides. It is a research and learning project, not a medical device, and it should not be used on patients.
  </p>

  <h2 id="away">The test it failed</h2>
  <p>
    Version 1 was a ResNet50 at 224 pixels. On its own held-out split of <Claim id="cf.test.n" /> images it reached macro-F1 <Claim id="cf.test.macroF1" /> and balanced accuracy <Claim id="cf2.v1.homeBA" />. Then I ran it on the official ISIC 2018 Task 3
    test set, 1,511 images checked against the training data for shared image IDs, shared lesion IDs and identical files.
    Balanced accuracy fell to <Claim id="cf2.v1.awayBA" />.
  </p>
  <HomeAway />
  <p>
    The safety layer was doing its job. It was putting honest error bars on a classifier that was not good enough. Everything
    after this point was about the classifier.
  </p>

  <h2 id="audit">Reading my own code</h2>
  <p>Before retraining anything, I read the code the way a reviewer would. Three things changed how I saw the metadata half of the project.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>The API invented a patient.</strong> The form fields defaulted to age 50, male, back, and the frontend pre-filled the
      same values. Anyone who skipped the form got a prediction for a person who does not exist. The fields are now required,
      "unknown" is accepted when someone chooses it, and every response echoes the metadata it used.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>"Unknown" was a diagnosis hint.</strong> In HAM10000 the rows with sex recorded as unknown are moles and benign keratoses,
      not one malignant, so a model that treats "unknown" as an ordinary category can learn it as a vote for benign. v1 had no safe default at all.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The metadata sees part of the answer.</strong> Age, sex and site alone, in a logistic regression, beat chance by a wide
      margin. That signal is real, and it is also the kind of dataset habit that may not travel to another clinic.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <CodeBlock code={meta} label="the change, simplified" del={[2]} add={[5]} />

  <h2 id="offsets">The cheap fix that wasn't</h2>
  <p>
    The cheapest way to raise balanced accuracy is to leave the model alone and shift its decision rule: one offset per class,
    fitted on validation logits, with melanoma recall not allowed to fall. The first fit took ISIC 2018 balanced accuracy to <Claim id="cf2.offsets.first" />.
  </p>
  <p>
    Refitting the same offsets on 20 bootstrap resamples of the validation set gave a median gain of <Claim id="cf2.offsets.median" />. The first
    result had been luck, and it cost several points of accuracy and melanoma precision. Offsets decide which mistakes you make.
    They cannot change how well the model ranks a melanoma above a mole, which is exactly what AUC measures, so the ceiling was in
    the features. I did not ship them.
  </p>

  <h2 id="v2">v2</h2>
  <p>
    v2 sits next to v1 in the repository and uses exactly the same split, so the two can be compared image by image. It changes the
    classifier and keeps the safety layer.
  </p>
  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><td></td><th scope="col">v1</th><th scope="col">v2</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th scope="row">Backbone</th><td>ResNet50</td><td>EfficientNetV2-S, ImageNet-21k</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Input</th><td>224 px, squashed from 600 × 450</td><td>384 px crops, aspect kept</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Colour</th><td>as captured</td><td>Shades of Gray colour constancy</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">At test time</th><td>one view</td><td>logits averaged over 8 flips and rotations</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Class weights</th><td>1/n</td><td>1/√n</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Optimiser</th><td>AdamW, constant rate</td><td>warmup, cosine decay, mixed precision, EMA weights</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Metadata</th><td>"unknown" as a category</td><td>missing flags, fields dropped at random in training</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Checkpoint chosen by</th><td>validation macro-F1</td><td>validation balanced accuracy</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    It trained on a free Kaggle T4 in about three minutes an epoch. On ISIC 2018 balanced accuracy went from <Claim id="cf2.v1.awayBA" /> to <Claim id="cf2.v2.awayBA" />,
    a paired gain of <Claim id="cf2.v2.delta" /> with an interval of <Claim id="cf2.v2.deltaCi" />. Every comparison is paired: both models scored on the
    same 1,511 images, with a bootstrap interval on the difference.
  </p>
  <p>
    The AUCs matter more to me than the headline, because they are the ranking itself improving, the one thing the offsets could
    not touch. Melanoma AUC rose from <Claim id="cf2.v1.melAUC" /> to <Claim id="cf2.v2.melAUC" />, and malignant-versus-benign AUC from <Claim id="cf2.v1.malAUC" /> to <Claim id="cf2.v2.malAUC" />.
    Melanoma recall also went up, but its interval, <Claim id="cf2.melRecall.deltaCi" />, crosses zero, so I do not claim it.
  </p>

  <h2 id="flag">The malignant flag</h2>
  <p>
    I refitted the safety layer for v2 from validation data only and applied it to ISIC 2018 unchanged. At nearly the same
    sensitivity, specificity rose from <Claim id="cf2.v1.spec" /> to <Claim id="cf2.v2.spec" />.
  </p>
  <FalseAlarms />
  <p>
    The conformal sets kept their promise on data they had never seen, covering the true class <Claim id="cf2.v2.coverage" /> of the time
    against a 0.90 target, while getting smaller: <Claim id="cf2.v2.setSize" /> classes on average instead of <Claim id="cf2.v1.setSize" />.
    v2's sensitivity sits slightly under its 0.95 target, and its interval still includes it.
  </p>

  <h2 id="fusion">Does the metadata earn the name?</h2>
  <p>
    I trained an image-only ablation with everything else identical. It scored <Claim id="cf2.imgOnly.awayBA" /> on ISIC 2018, and fusion beat it by <Claim id="cf2.fusion.vsImg" />, interval <Claim id="cf2.fusion.vsImgCi" />.
  </p>
  <p>
    Most of that gap does not come from the form. With all metadata withheld, the fusion model still scores <Claim id="cf2.fusion.noForm" />.
    Giving the form back adds <Claim id="cf2.fusion.form" />, with an interval of <Claim id="cf2.fusion.formCi" /> that crosses zero. Either training with metadata
    and field dropout made the image branch better, or the image-only run drew an unlucky seed. With one finished run of each I cannot
    separate the two yet, and I would rather say that than call it a fusion win.
  </p>

  <h2 id="silent">The silent failure</h2>
  <p>
    Earlier, v1 predicted the same class for every image and nothing raised an error. The loader used <code>strict=False</code> and was loading a stale image-only checkpoint into the fusion model. Weights that did not match were
    skipped without a word, and the predictions collapsed.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={bug} label="the shape of the bug" del={[2]} />
  <CodeBlock code={fixed} label="the loud version" add={[2, 3]} />
  <p class="small muted"><span class="illustrative">Illustrative.</span> <code>load_state_dict</code> returns the missing and unexpected keys. With <code>strict=False</code> nobody reads them unless you ask.</p>
  <p>
    The fix had two parts: <a href={fix}>track the correct fusion checkpoint and drop the stale one</a>, and <a href={loud}>make loading print missing and unexpected key counts instead of failing silently</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="calibration">Calibration</h2>
  <p>
    A softmax output is often more confident than it is right. Temperature scaling divides every logit by one number, <em>T</em>, before
    the softmax. For v1 I <a href={temperature}>fitted it on the validation set</a> by minimising negative log-likelihood and got <em>T</em> = <Claim id="cf.temperature" />.
    v2 gets its own temperature the same way, and its calibration error on ISIC 2018 is <Claim id="cf2.v2.ece" />.
  </p>
  <TemperatureDemo />

  <h2 id="latency">Latency</h2>
  <p>
    I profiled v1 inference phase by phase. The backward pass for Grad-CAM dominated each request, so explanations are opt-in. Without
    one, local latency drops from <Claim id="cf.latency.explain" /> to <Claim id="cf.latency.fast" /> per request, on one CPU worker. Those are local numbers, not production ones.
  </p>

  <h2 id="limits">What this doesn't show</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>v2 is not served yet.</strong> The API still runs v1. v2 needs its checkpoint hosted and the out-of-distribution guard refitted on its features.</li>
    <li><strong>Training-seed variance is unmeasured.</strong> A second fusion run was interrupted. The intervals above cover test-set sampling, not the luck of a training run.</li>
    <li><strong>Phone photos are untested.</strong> The guard was checked against everyday photos and synthetic patterns, not pictures of skin taken on a phone.</li>
    <li><strong>Skin tone cannot be measured here.</strong> HAM10000 skews toward lighter skin and records no skin type.</li>
    <li>There is no always-on public demo.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="reproduce">Reproduce</h2>
  <p>Every v2 number is recomputed from logits committed to the project repository. No GPU and no images needed.</p>
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" label="terminal" code={`git clone https://github.com/Avnish1505/cancer-fusion-ai
cd cancer-fusion-ai
python -m src.analyze_v2`} />
  <p>The v1 test-set numbers on this page are recomputed by a script in this site's repository:</p>
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" label="in this site's repository" code={`pip install numpy
python scripts/derive/cancer_fusion_metrics.py`} />
</CaseStudy>
